fit_content = true

[node name="2" type="RichTextLabel" parent="CenterContainer/book/MarginContainer/HBoxContainer/right/VBoxContainer/right" unique_id=1170668357]
visible = false
layout_mode = 2
size_flags_vertical = 6
bbcode_enabled = true
text = "One would be a fool to teach what he himself knows not, so this booklet shall only focus on a particular member of the extensive Elvish language family. This is, coincidentally, also the form of Elvish you, dear reader, are likely to encounter. 

This language is called Sindarin, and was originally spoken by the Sindar, or Grey Elves. It has since, however, spread across the entirety of Middle-Earth.
Usually, Sindari is written down using the Tengwar script, which looks a little like this:

[center][font=res://fonts/tengwar_annatar/tnganb.ttf]5^È 7x%P 1`N 7j&L 3t$ j#°[/font][/center]

It is, however, quite painful to write for my old, tired, hands. Therefore, I've decided to not include a guide to reading Tengwar. The reader can acquire one of these at any book-shop. Additionally, this booklet shall focus on the spoken form of the language, being centralised on pronounciation rather than grammar or similar abstract nonsense.
 
However, be warned: it's been ages since I've used Sindarin in conversation!"
fit_content = true

[node name="3" type="RichTextLabel" parent="CenterContainer/book/MarginContainer/HBoxContainer/right/VBoxContainer/right" unique_id=420910431]
visible = false
layout_mode = 2
size_flags_vertical = 6
bbcode_enabled = true
text = "[font_size=40]WHAT[/font_size] follows is, to my knowledge. a list of words in Sindarin, along with their meanings.

In real situations, you might notice slight alterations due to dialectical differences or grammar.

One grammatical rule you are particularly likely to struggle with is this:
words can sometimes undergo soft mutation, where the first con-sonant of the word changes. This occurs for example if a noun is a direct object, follows an article, an adjective following a noun or a negative form with the prefix ú-. The pattern is as follows:

p=>b t=>d c=>g b=>v d=>dh g=>’ s=>h h=>ch

The vowels of a verb or a plural noun can also change, depending on the form.

So very complicated, isn't it? I've asked Elves why they wouldn't change it to something more sensible, but they would not answer. Queer!
"
fit_content = true

[node name="4" type="RichTextLabel" parent="CenterContainer/book/MarginContainer/HBoxContainer/right/VBoxContainer/right" unique_id=1270626511]
visible = false
layout_mode = 2
size_flags_vertical = 6
bbcode_enabled = true
text = "[font_size=30]D[/font_size]

Dod : berry
Drinna- : to twist

[font_size=30]E[/font_size]

Edhel: elf
Elefhrim: elves (race)
Eno: still

[font_size=30]F[/font_size]

Fel-: to feel
Fesga-: to itch
Fir-: to die

[font_size=30]G[/font_size]

Gar-: to have, hold
Gerf: knife
Gobem: mouth
Gonn: rock
Groga: to feel fear


"
fit_content = true

[node name="5" type="RichTextLabel" parent="CenterContainer/book/MarginContainer/HBoxContainer/right/VBoxContainer/right" unique_id=256945880]
visible = false
layout_mode = 2
size_flags_vertical = 6
bbcode_enabled = true
text = "[font_size=30]O[/font_size]

Olost: lazy
Othovor: too much

[font_size=30]P[/font_size]

Perian: hobbit
Pol- : to be able to
Pruin : charcoal

[font_size=30]R[/font_size]

Rag- : burst, break

[font_size=30]S[/font_size]

Saeb: hungry
San: it
San- : to think (of)
Sen: this
Sing: salt

[font_size=30]T[/font_size]
